  "query_preview": "..A trough over the west coast will continue to\nproduce southwesterly flow over our region over the next few days.\nDrier air in the mid levels has also moved into northern Arizona,\nthough sufficient low level moisture remains (dewpoints 45-55F)\nfor a few showers and thunderstorms, primarily over the higher\nterrain of Coconino County. Isolated strong storms will be\npossible with the increased wind shear, mainly in northwest\nArizona. Some very localized areas of heavy rainfall will be \npossible through early this evening as well. Breezy southwest \nwinds will also develop each day, in the 15-20 mph range with \ngusts 25-30 mph. Daytime highs will be a few degrees cooler than \nnormal. Starting as early as Thursday evening, models are \nindicating an increase in moisture from the south and higher \nchances for showers and thunderstorms. This should be in part due \nto a moisture surge up the Gulf of California as a tropical system\nmoves well offshore the tip of Baja into the weekend (official \nNHC forecast track for TS Marie). There is still some uncertainty \nin where the deepest moisture tracks (AZ or farther west), but the\nincrease will be enough to bring higher shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage Friday through the holiday weekend. Our current forecasts\nindicate 30-60% chances each day, highest over higher terrain. \nThis could also include some overnight storm activity. Daily high \ntemperatures through the holiday weekend are currently forecast to\nbe near to a few degrees below normal, but will be highly \ndependent on storm coverage.",
